+  IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
+  FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE
+  AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
+  LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,
+  OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
+  SOFTWARE.
+*/
+
+// Regression test for CVE-2026-84384 / GHSA-24wx-9w62-c96w.
+//
+// libheif decompresses "deflate"-encoded metadata items (mime/Exif/XMP) via
+// metadata_compression.cc::inflate(). Before the fix, that function grew its
+// output vector with no accounting of the total decompressed size, so a small
+// crafted zlib payload could expand into gigabytes of output and exhaust memory
+// (CWE-409 data amplification / decompression-bomb DoS).
+//
+// The upstream fix (v1.23.2) targets the newer compression_zlib.cc/brotli code
+// paths and the heif_security_limits API, none of which exist in the 1.15.1
+// codebase shipped by Debian bookworm. This target-native test therefore
+// exercises the actual 1.15.1 code path -- the internal inflate() routine -- and
+// verifies that:
+//   (1) a genuine zlib "bomb" (a tiny compressed buffer that would inflate to
+//       well over the cap) is rejected: inflate() returns an empty vector rather
+//       than allocating unbounded memory; and
+//   (2) a normal, small compressed payload still round-trips correctly, proving
+//       the cap does not break legitimate metadata decompression.
+//
+// The test is compiled only when deflate/inflate support is built in, matching
+// the WITH_DEFLATE_HEADER_COMPRESSION guard around the code under test.
+
+#include "catch.hpp"
+
+#if WITH_DEFLATE_HEADER_COMPRESSION
+
+#include "libheif/metadata_compression.h"
+
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <vector>
+
+TEST_CASE("zlib decompression bomb is bounded", "[metadata_compression]")
+{
+  // Build a highly compressible input larger than the decompression-output cap
+  // enforced by inflate() (50 MiB). Compressing a run of identical bytes yields
+  // only a few KB, so the compressed form is the classic "bomb": tiny in, huge
+  // out.
+  const size_t rawSize = 64u * 1024u * 1024u; // 64 MiB > 50 MiB cap
+  std::vector<uint8_t> raw(rawSize, 0);
+
+  std::vector<uint8_t> bomb = deflate(raw.data(), static_cast<int>(raw.size()));
+  REQUIRE_FALSE(bomb.empty());
+  // Sanity: the payload really is small relative to what it expands to.
+  REQUIRE(bomb.size() < rawSize / 100);
+
+  // Before the fix this call would try to materialize ~64 MiB (and, for a real
+  // adversarial stream, far more) with no bound. After the fix, inflate() aborts
+  // once the accumulated output exceeds the cap and returns an empty vector
+  // (the function's existing error convention).
+  std::vector<uint8_t> inflated = inflate(bomb);
+  REQUIRE(inflated.empty());
+}
+
+TEST_CASE("zlib decompression of legitimate metadata still works", "[metadata_compression]")
+{
+  // A small, realistic metadata payload must still round-trip: the cap must not
+  // reject valid, non-amplifying data.
+  std::vector<uint8_t> original;
+  original.reserve(4096);
+  for (size_t i = 0; i < 4096; i++) {
+    original.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>((i * 37 + 11) & 0xFF));
+  }
+
+  std::vector<uint8_t> compressed = deflate(original.data(), static_cast<int>(original.size()));
+  REQUIRE_FALSE(compressed.empty());
+
+  std::vector<uint8_t> decompressed = inflate(compressed);
+  REQUIRE(decompressed == original);
+}
+
+#endif // WITH_DEFLATE_HEADER_COMPRESSION
Description: CVE-2026-84447 - Bound derived-image reference amplification
 Crafted grid/iovl/iden reference graphs can repeatedly decode the same base
 image because derived-image decoding has no shared operation budget and the
 per-path cycle-detection set forks at every recursion branch. Nested
 references make the number of decodes grow as branch^depth, so a tiny file
 can request an effectively unbounded amount of decoding work (CPU/memory
 amplification DoS, CWE-770).
 .
 This is a root-cause backport of upstream commit
 30b52a4e829efe0ee6d7208e8500b99f664af5e9 (GHSA-x8xm-cm2c-cfc8), which is
 structurally inapplicable to 1.15.1's monolithic heif_context.cc (the fix was
 written against a later ImageItem-based architecture). The backport
 reimplements the same three defenses directly against 1.15.1's
 HeifContext decode call chain:
 .
  - A shared decode-operation budget (a shared_ptr<atomic> in a new
    DecodeTraversalState, threaded by value through the decode recursion and
    the parallel tile-decode threads), checked at the single choke point
    HeifContext::decode_image_planar and capped at a small multiple of the
    number of image items in the file. This is the structure-agnostic backstop
    against the exponential blow-up.
  - A per-path overlay-nesting depth guard for fast rejection of the nested
    'iovl' gadget.
  - A per-overlay fan-out limit (MAX_OVERLAY_IMAGES) in decode_overlay_image.
 .
 The public C API and ABI are unaffected; only internal C++ signatures change
 (a defaulted DecodeTraversalState parameter). The upstream file-open
 white/gray/black DFS change to HeifFile::check_for_ref_cycle_recursion is not
 applicable: 1.15.1 has no such cycle check at file-open, and the decode budget
 already bounds the corresponding decode-time cost. The 'tili'/V3 tiled path is
 not implemented in 1.15.1 and is therefore out of scope.
 .
 A regression test (tests/overlay_amplification.cc), backported and adapted to
 the 1.15.1 autotools/catch.hpp test harness, verifies that a deeply nested
 overlay gadget and an over-wide overlay are both rejected quickly with a
 security-limit error, while a shallow legitimate overlay is not falsely
 rejected.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/commit/30b52a4e829efe0ee6d7208e8500b99f664af5e9
Bug: https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/security/advisories/GHSA-x8xm-cm2c-cfc8
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: Patch Platoon <security@patch-platoon.example>
Last-Update: 2026-09-22
---
--- a/libheif/heif_limits.h
+++ b/libheif/heif_limits.h
@@ -46,4 +46,32 @@
 static const int64_t MAX_FILE_POS = 0x007FFFFFFFFFFFFFLL; // maximum file position
 static const int MAX_FRACTION_VALUE = 0x10000;
 
+// --- Derived-image reference-amplification bounds.
+// (CVE-2026-84447 / GHSA-x8xm-cm2c-cfc8)
+//
+// Derived images (grid/iovl/iden) can reference the same base image through
+// indirection. Since decoding has no result cache, a shared subtree is decoded
+// once per path that reaches it, and nested references make the number of
+// decodes grow as branch^depth -- a tiny file can request an effectively
+// unbounded amount of decoding work. The constants below bound that.
+
+// Maximum number of 'iovl' overlay images nested inside one another along a
+// single decode path. Real files use at most one overlay per decode chain, so
+// this is a structural sanity limit, not a common-case constraint.
+static const uint32_t MAX_OVERLAY_NESTING_LEVEL = 3;
+
+// Maximum number of input images that a single 'iovl' overlay may composite.
+static const uint32_t MAX_OVERLAY_IMAGES = 5;
+
+// The total number of sub-image decode operations triggered by one top-level
+// decode is bounded to (number of image items in the file) * this factor. A
+// well-formed file decodes each of its items a small number of times, so this
+// only trips on reference amplification.
+static const uint32_t MAX_DERIVED_IMAGE_DECODE_FACTOR = 2;
+
+// Lower bound for that budget, so that files with very few items still have a
+// little headroom (e.g. an image plus its alpha auxiliary). Negligible for
+// amplification, which is either exponential or scales with the item count.
+static const uint32_t MIN_DERIVED_IMAGE_DECODE_BUDGET = 100;
+
 #endif  // LIBHEIF_HEIF_LIMITS_H
