+**CVE-2026-32741 is a false positive for Debian 12 (Bookworm).** Although scanners flag `libheif` 1.15.1 based on a broad version range, the vulnerable `MaskImageCodec::decode_mask_image()` implementation and associated `mski` decoding support were introduced only in later upstream releases. Since the vulnerable code does not exist in Bookworm's source tree, the package should be classified as **not affected** with the justification **`vulnerable_code_not_present`**.
+
+## CVE-2026-41069 — False Positive for Debian 12 (Bookworm)
+
+| Field                 | Value                                                                                                                 |
+| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| **CVE ID**            | CVE-2026-41069                                                                                                        |
+| **Component**         | libheif                                                                                                               |
+| **CWE**               | CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read                                                                                           |
+| **Distribution**      | Debian 12 (Bookworm)                                                                                                  |
+| **Bookworm package**  | `libheif` source package, version `1.15.1-1` / `1.15.1-1+deb12u1` (security/point-release respins on the 1.15.1 base) |
+| **Upstream affected** | libheif ≤ 1.21.2 *that ship `SampleAuxInfoReader` / the sequences-track subsystem* (introduced in 1.20.0)             |
+| **Upstream fixed in** | libheif 1.22.0 (commit `71755d3d41a117685a3274bdd1214fc50a760f20`, "fix: reject malformed sequence files with saiz samples but no chunks") |
+| **Assessment**        | **FALSE POSITIVE for Bookworm — package is not vulnerable, no patch required.**                                       |
+
+---
+
+> This is a companion issue to **CVE-2026-41071** (above). Both live in the same
+> `SampleAuxInfoReader` constructor in `libheif/sequences/track.cc`, but they are
+> **distinct root causes fixed by two separate commits** made ~20 minutes apart on the
+> same day:
+>
+> * CVE-2026-41071 — `current_chunk` walks *past* `chunks.size()` as it increments across
+>   a crafted sample table (fixed by `f20c8174`, replacing a release-mode no-op `assert`
+>   with a real bounds check that breaks the loop).
+> * CVE-2026-41069 (this entry) — `chunks` is *empty from the very first iteration*
+>   because `stco.entry_count == 0`. `saio.entry_count == 0` then satisfies the
+>   "`num_chunks == 1` or `num_chunks == m_stco->get_number_of_chunks()`" check trivially
+>   (`0 == 0`), so the file passes validation even though `saiz.sample_count > 0`. The
+>   constructor still enters its per-sample loop and evaluates `chunks[current_chunk]`
+>   (i.e. `chunks[0]`) on the very first iteration, before the increment/bounds-check path
+>   from CVE-2026-41071's fix is ever reached — an out-of-bounds read on an empty vector.
+
+### 1. Why this CVE is being flagged
+
+Scanners (Trivy, Grype, OSV-Scanner, Anchore, etc.) and the Debian security tracker match this CVE against any package whose version compares as `<= 1.21.2`. The Bookworm package version `1.15.1-1` satisfies that comparison, so it is flagged.
+
+**Version comparison alone is incorrect here.** The CVE describes a defect in the `SampleAuxInfoReader` constructor in the image-sequences subsystem, and neither that function, that file, nor the entire sequences/track feature exists in the 1.15.1 source tree shipped by Bookworm. The advisory's "≤ 1.21.2" range therefore produces a false match against 1.15.1.
+
+### 2. Why Bookworm's libheif 1.15.1 is not vulnerable
+
+The CVE describes an out-of-bounds read triggered when a HEIF sequence file has an empty chunk-offset table (`stco.entry_count == 0`) but a non-empty sample auxiliary info table (`saiz.sample_count > 0`):
+
+```cpp
+// libheif/sequences/track.cc — DOES NOT EXIST IN 1.15.1
+SampleAuxInfoReader::SampleAuxInfoReader(std::shared_ptr<Box_saiz> saiz,
+                                         std::shared_ptr<Box_saio> saio,
+                                         const std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Chunk>>& chunks)
+{
+  bool oneChunk = (saio->get_num_chunks() == 1);   // 0 chunks -> oneChunk == false
+  uint32_t current_chunk = 0;
+  uint32_t nSamples = saiz->get_num_samples();      // attacker-controlled, > 0
+  ...
+  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < nSamples; i++) {
+    if (!oneChunk && i > chunks[current_chunk]->last_sample_number()) {  // chunks[0] on empty vector
+      ...
+    }
+    ...
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Before the fix, the caller (`Track::load()`) only checked that `saio->get_num_chunks()` was `1` or matched `m_stco->get_number_of_chunks()` — a check that a *jointly* zero `stco`/`saio` entry count trivially satisfies (`0 == 0`), even though `m_chunks` (built from `stco`) is empty and `saiz` still declares samples. The fix (`71755d3d`) adds an explicit guard in `Track::load()`:
+
+```cpp
+if (saio->get_num_chunks() != 1 && m_chunks.empty() && saiz->get_num_samples() > 0) {
+  return Error{
+    heif_error_Invalid_input, heif_suberror_Unspecified,
+    "'saiz' box references samples but no chunks exist."
+  };
+}
+```
+
+which rejects the file *before* `SampleAuxInfoReader` is ever constructed, so `chunks[0]` is never dereferenced on an empty vector.
+
+For this bug to be reachable, **all of the following must be true**:
+
+1. The image-sequences subsystem exists.
+2. `libheif/sequences/track.cc` and the `Track` class exist.
+3. Sample Auxiliary Information (`saiz` / `saio`) parsing exists via `SampleAuxInfoReader`.
+4. A crafted file can have `stco.entry_count == 0` and `saio.entry_count == 0` (so `m_chunks` is empty) while `saiz.sample_count > 0`.
+
+In libheif 1.15.1 **none of these conditions are met**. The entire sequences/track subsystem did not exist.
+
+#### 2.1 Direct evidence — the file and symbols do not exist
+
+```bash
+$ git ls-tree -r v1.15.1 --name-only | grep -iE 'sequences|track\.cc|saiz|saio|stco'
+(empty — no results)
+```
+
+```bash
+$ git grep -nE 'SampleAuxInfoReader|class Box_saiz|class Box_saio|class Box_stco|class Track' v1.15.1 -- '*.cc' '*.h'
+(empty — no results)
+```
+
+There is no `libheif/sequences/` directory, no `track.cc`, no `SampleAuxInfoReader`, no `saiz`/`saio`/`stco` parser, and no track or sequence decode path anywhere in the Bookworm source tree.
+
+#### 2.2 Upstream history — when the vulnerable code actually landed
+
+| Upstream milestone | What it added                                                                                                                        | First tagged release |
+| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------- |
+| Sequences API       | Introduced image-sequences support, track reading/writing, chunk offsets (`stco`), Sample Auxiliary Information (`saiz` / `saio`), and `SampleAuxInfoReader` | **1.20.0**            |
+| `71755d3d`          | CVE-2026-41069 fix ("reject malformed sequence files with saiz samples but no chunks")                                               | **1.22.0**            |
+| `f20c8174`          | CVE-2026-41071 fix (companion issue, fixed ~20 minutes later the same day)                                                           | **1.22.0**            |
+
+Bookworm shipped from the 1.15.1 base, which predates the sequences API by **five minor releases**. The vulnerable function did not exist in 1.15.1.
+
+#### 2.3 Runtime behaviour with a malicious sequence file
+
+When libheif 1.15.1 encounters a HEIF/MP4-style file containing image-sequence tracks with a crafted `stco`/`saio`/`saiz` combination:
+
+* No sequence/track reader exists.
+* No code parses `stco`, `saiz`, or `saio`.
+* No `SampleAuxInfoReader` exists.
+* The vulnerable loop does not exist.
+
+A proof-of-concept file that triggers the vulnerability on 1.21.x cannot reach the vulnerable code path on Bookworm's 1.15.1 release.
+
+### 3. How to verify locally
+
+```bash
+# Confirm package version
+$ dpkg-query -W -f='${Version}\n' libheif1
+1.15.1-1
+
+# Or point release
+1.15.1-1+deb12u1
+
+# Confirm source has no sequence subsystem
+$ apt-get source libheif
+$ cd libheif-1.15.1
+
+$ find . -iname '*track*' -o -iname '*sequence*'
+# no results
+
+$ grep -RE 'SampleAuxInfoReader|fourcc\("saiz"\)|fourcc\("stco"\)' .
+# no results
+
+$ grep -RE 'class Track' .
+# no results
+```
+
+If these searches return no matches, the vulnerable code path is absent.
+
+### 4. Recommended action
+
+#### Do not patch
+
+There is nothing to patch because the vulnerable function and the entire subsystem do not exist in this source tree.
+
+#### Mark as not affected
+
+Suggested metadata:
+
+```yaml
+status: not_affected
+justification: vulnerable_code_not_present
+impact_statement: >
+  libheif 1.15.1 (Bookworm) predates the introduction of the
+  image-sequences/track subsystem, chunk-offset (stco) parsing, and Sample
+  Auxiliary Information (saiz/saio) parsing. The vulnerable
+  SampleAuxInfoReader constructor was introduced in upstream 1.20.0.
+  Neither the class, the file libheif/sequences/track.cc, nor any
+  saiz/saio/stco decode path exists in this version, making the reported
+  out-of-bounds read unreachable.
+```
+
+#### Example VEX / OpenVEX statement
+
+```json
+{
+  "vulnerability": "CVE-2026-41069",
+  "products": [
+    "pkg:deb/debian/libheif@1.15.1-1?distro=bookworm"
+  ],
+  "status": "not_affected",
+  "justification": "vulnerable_code_not_present"
+}
+```
+
+For Trivy, this can also be represented using a `.trivyignore` entry with a justification comment, although a VEX document is preferred.
+
+> **Note on Debian patch series:** This assessment is based on upstream libheif 1.15.1. Debian's `1.15.1-1+deb12u1` includes a small patch series, but there is no indication that the entire 1.20.0 sequences subsystem was backported. If strict verification is required, inspect the Debian patch set and confirm that no patch introduces `libheif/sequences/` or `SampleAuxInfoReader`.
+
+### 5. References
+
+* Upstream fix commit: https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/commit/71755d3d41a117685a3274bdd1214fc50a760f20
+* Companion fix (CVE-2026-41071), same day: https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/commit/f20c81745e917b4c496615140385c86d7a2fa58d
+* Upstream introduction of the sequences API / SAI (`saiz`/`saio`/`stco`) handling: libheif 1.20.0 release — https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/releases/tag/v1.20.0
+* CVE record: https://vulnerability.circl.lu/vuln/cve-2026-41069
